# Architecture: one LLM split across many browser tabs

Unstable Legion is **not** "each peer runs its own chatbot." It is ONE
distributed Qwen3-8B whose 36 transformer layers are **split across peers**
(pipeline parallelism over WebRTC). A prompt is tokenized locally, hidden-state
**activations** stream stage-to-stage across the mesh, and the final stage
streams tokens back. Keep this mental model — most bugs come from thinking of
peers as independent chatbots.

## The pipeline

- **Driver** = the tab asking a question. A **capable driver** hosts embeddings
  + first layers `[0, driverLayers)` locally in WebGPU; a **thin driver** (no
  usable WebGPU) hosts nothing and relies on a remote `isFirst` host. Same state
  machine, a mode flag — not a fork (`docs/OPTIONAL-STAGE0.md`).
- **Communal hosts** each load a **contiguous** layer range and serve it. They
  self-assemble to cover `[0, 36)` (`packages/mesh-core/src/communalAssembly.ts`)
  — no central assignment.
- Only boundary **activations** cross the wire (f16 default — `docs/WIRE-DTYPE.md`),
  one header per stream + one frame per token. KV cache stays on each host.
- The driver plans a route (`stagePlanner.ts` / `communalTopology.ts`) and the
  orchestrator (`stageOrchestrator.ts`) attaches stages, with **attach-time
  candidate failover** and **mid-stream stall → continue-from-history replan**.

## Key modules (mesh-core)

- `peer.ts` — trystero peer, wire actions, roster, ICE diagnostics install.
- `guards.ts` — runtime validation of every inbound wire message (see the
  mesh-protocol rule; additive idiom is load-bearing).
- `communalAssembly.ts` — a host decides which layer range to claim/grow/shrink.
- `communalTopology.ts` — flatten roster `loadedStages` → coverage/gaps/route.
- `stagePlanner.ts` — capacity + stability ranking of candidate hosts.
- `stageOrchestrator.ts` — drive a session: attach, prefill, decode, replan.
- `stageControl.ts` — stage session control frames (ping/open/token/stop).
- `hostHealth.ts` — driver-side demotion tracker (cooldown/backoff) — WIP wiring.

## Known resilience gaps (be careful, don't regress)

- A stage assigned to an unresponsive host stalls that request. Failover exists
  **only when another candidate covers the same range**. A single host camping a
  **center** range can leave a non-contiguous gap the survivors can't tile — the
  driver's replan only stitches *already-loaded* ranges, it can't force a reload.
  Design direction: bridge with temporary multi-segment hosting, then heal slowly
  toward a contiguous split; demote blockers with hysteresis (don't thrash).
- One incompatible/broken peer must not sink the mesh. That's why guards are
  additive and why host-health demotion exists.

## Repo shape

`packages/mesh-core` (framework-free TS) → `packages/mesh-react` (hooks:
`useCommunalChat`, `useCommunalHost`, `useStageHost`) → `apps/chat` (THE product,
`legion.codecai.net/`) and `apps/demo` (`/classic/`). The sliced llama.cpp
WebGPU **stage runtime** is the sibling `legion-stage-runtime` repo.
